[agent] ## What I did (all platform-side via the `hops` CLI)
[agent] 1. **Logged the predictions on the platform** — created feature group `prediction_log` (v1) in the feature store and inserted all 13,505 logged predictions from `data/prediction_log.csv` (`ts`, `prediction`). Hopsworks computed insert statistics on ingest.
[agent] 2. **Monitored the distribution over time** — ran the platform's SQL engine (`hops sql`) against the feature group's offline table to compute per-day descriptive statistics (count, mean, stddev). The offline FG turned out to be a Delta Lake table, so I queried it through the `delta` catalog in the `_featurestore` schema.
[agent] 3. **Identified the shift onset** — daily mean prediction is stable at **~4.5–4.7 (sd ~0.8)** every day from 2026-01-01 through **2026-02-25**, then jumps to **~7.77** on **2026-02-26** and stays at ~7.7–7.9 for the rest of the log. An hourly drill-down across the boundary confirmed the step is sharp and lands exactly at the start of 2026-02-26 (last hours of 02-25 ≈ 4.2–5.1; first hours of 02-26 ≈ 7.5–8.4).
[agent] **Onset of the distribution shift: 2026-02-26.**
[agent] One interface note worth flagging: the CLI-inserted feature group's offline storage is a Delta table, but `hops sql` defaults to the `iceberg` catalog and the default schema is the project name rather than the `<project>_featurestore` schema where FG tables live. Getting an aggregation query to run required discovering the right catalog (`delta`) and schema via `SHOW CATALOGS`/`SHOW SCHEMAS` — the CLI gives no direct hint that FG offline tables aren't reachable from the default `hops sql` catalog/schema.
